AB  - In recent years, the emergence of Large Language Models (LLMs) has transformed the landscape of higher education. Among their most promising applications is the potential to personalise student learning and serve as virtual tutors. Yet for accounting educators, the key practical question is not simply whether LLMs can answer accounting questions, but how reliably they perform across different accounting tasks and what this implies for their possible use in teaching. This paper examines the reliability and pedagogical relevance of the most widely-used LLMs – ChatGPT, Gemini, CoPilot, Claude, Meta AI and DeepSeek – in introductory accounting. We evaluate their accuracy on theoretical and practical multiple-choice questions drawn from real university exams, test whether simple prompting strategies improve performance, and compare advanced reasoning versions with their freely available baselines. The results show that LLMs perform strongly on theoretical questions, less consistently on journal entries, and less reliably on financial statement preparation. Prompting strategies produce mixed effects, whereas advanced reasoning models show clearer improvements. We also complement the model-based analysis with a pilot classroom study in which undergraduate accounting students were randomly assigned to receive support from either an instructor or an LLM during an income statement preparation task. The pilot study provides preliminary empirical evidence that, in a short and focused activity, LLM-supported work may lead to performance levels similar to those observed under instructor-supported conditions. Overall, the findings suggest that current LLMs may already offer useful support in some areas of introductory accounting, but their reliability remains uneven across task types.
