AB  - The rapid integration of generative artificial intelligence (AI) in higher education raises concerns about “illusory competence,” wherein students produce polished work without demonstrating authentic understanding. This issue is particularly consequential in perinatal nursing education, where safe practice depends on sound clinical reasoning and adaptive judgment. This article describes the implementation and evaluation of a structured verbal assessment designed to promote authentic learning and safeguard academic integrity. Students completed a 45-minute, face-to-face assessment requiring real-time articulation of clinical reasoning in response to complex perinatal case scenarios. A program evaluation survey of 25 students included Likert-scale and open-ended items assessing engagement, skill development, and perceived value. Results demonstrated high engagement and improved topic comprehension. Qualitative feedback identified enhanced knowledge application and deeper retention, alongside challenges related to anxiety and question clarity. Iterative refinements improved structure and consistency. Structured verbal assessment represents a feasible, AI-resilient strategy for strengthening clinical decision-making in nursing education.
