AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly embedded in academic writing instruction, yet empirical evidence on its learning effects and the ways those effects are evaluated remains fragmented. Following PRISMA guidelines, this systematic review searched the Web of Science Core Collection (SCI, SSCI, and A&HCI) with no date restrictions (final search: October 31, 2025), screened 341 records, and synthesised 25 empirical studies. The review mapped AI tool use and functions across writing stages, documented evaluation domains and measurement approaches, and summarised reported effects. Across studies, large language model chatbots were primarily used for idea generation, outlining, and early drafting, whereas automated writing evaluation and automated written feedback tools were more common in revision and feedback, with a smaller set of systems supporting assessment and scoring. Evaluation targeted three domains—psychological outcomes, writing processes, and textual products—with growing use of validated scales and process measures but continued reliance on general-purpose rubrics for textual assessment. Reported effects were more consistently positive for psychological and process outcomes than for product outcomes. Improvements in textual products were most evident in surface-level accuracy and conventions, while evidence for higher-order development (e.g., argumentation and discourse organisation) was mixed. Notably, the evidence base is dominated by course-based writing tasks and broad rubrics, which constrains inferences about genre- and discipline-sensitive development in research-oriented writing.
