AB  - With the widespread application of artificial intelligence (AI) technology in education, understanding the mechanisms through which AI influences college students' creativity has become a critical issue. Existing research predominantly analyzes this relationship from a singular perspective such as technological efficacy or cognitive psychology, lacking an integrated theoretical framework and in-depth exploration from the students' viewpoint. Thus, this study employed an exploratory sequential mixed-methods approach. First, through a grounded theory analysis of in-depth interviews with 10 college students, a conceptual model of AI’s impact on creativity was constructed based on the stimulus-organism-response (SOR) theory. Subsequently, a measurement scale was developed and validated through a questionnaire survey of 345 university students, using PLS-SEM to test the model paths. The findings reveal that efficiency, restraints, initiative, and outer assistance significantly promote emergent creativity through chained mediating effects involving integrated framework and mobilizing. Among these, initiative exerts the strongest influence on integrated framework (β = 0.521), while integrated framework significantly impacts mobilizing (β = 0.646). This study provides a potential theoretical framework for understanding human-machine collaborative creativity mechanisms and offers empirical evidence for educators designing AI-enhanced creativity development programs, thus providing possible guidance for designing AI-augmented learning environments that foster creativity.
