AB  - In a rapidly evolving digital society, higher education must equip students with essential twenty-first century skills such as creativity and digital literacy. This study explored the educational potential of TikTok through an active learning experience implemented in a “Foundations of Body Expression” course within a sport sciences degree program. A quantitative, quasi-experimental pre-test/post-test design, complemented by a qualitative descriptive component based on students’ responses to one open-ended question was used to investigate the perceptions of 156 university students regarding TikTok’s impact on their creativity, digital skills, and overall learning engagement. Quantitative findings showed statistically significant improvements in all variables, with self-perceived creativity displaying the greatest perceived growth. The qualitative analysis identified five thematic areas: (1) learning and creativity, (2) psychological and social benefits, (3) teaching methods and tools, (4) promotion and digital skills, and (5) critical considerations and warnings. Students described TikTok as an engaging, accessible, and motivating tool that promoted creative expression, active participation, and digital skills development. They particularly valued its integration of music, rhythm, and visual effects, which enhanced their ability to communicate and express through movement. Some students also noted potential risks such as distraction or misalignment with academic objectives. Overall, the findings support TikTok’s potential to enrich university teaching when framed within a student-centred, pedagogically sound approach.
