AB  - This study explores the advancing integration of Learning Analytics (LA) and Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning (CSCL) within the Flipped Classroom model in higher education, employing a systematic bibliometric approach. Drawing 152 Scopus-indexed articles using R/Bibliometrix and VOSviewer, the analysis validates a notable increase in research activity since 2019, highlighting LA's essential function in facilitating effective collaborative environments. Co-word network analysis identifies three highly active and interconnected thematic clusters: the focus on Self-Regulated Learning Metrics, the use of Multimodal Data Analysis, and the emergence of AI-Driven Feedback Systems. This thematic shift indicates a maturation of the field, moving from measuring basic engagement to analyzing the quality and cognitive complexity of collaborative interactions. The study concludes with an analytical framework for researchers and educators, positing that the future effectiveness of the Flipped CSCL model depends on the strategic application of data-driven insights to promote student agency and significantly improve the quality of peer-to-peer learning.
